es, syllables, letters, words or base pairs according to the application. The n-grams typically are collected from a text or speech corpus. When the items are words, n-grams may also be called shingles.[1]  An n-gram of size 1 is referred to as a \"unigram\"; size 2 is a \"bigram\" (or, less commonly, a \"digram\"); size 3 is a \"trigram\". Larger sizes are sometimes referred to by the value of n, e.g., \"four-gram\", \"five-gram\", and so on.","topics":[],"score":213132,"global_rank":252721,"on_pypi":false,"top_pypi":false,"python_rank":41048},{"name":"singh-karanpal/cookiecutter_exercise","owner":"singh-karanpal","html_url":"https://github.com/singh-karanpal/cookiecutter_exercise","stars":0,"forks":0,"watchers":0,"issues":0,"language":"Python","description":null,"topics":[],"score":213131,"global_rank":252733,"on_pypi":false,"top_pypi":false,"python_rank":41049},{"name":"puntara/Logical-expression-comparision","owner":"puntara","html_url":"https://github.com/puntara/Logical-expression-comparision","stars":0,"forks":0,"watchers":0,"issues":0,"language":"Python","description":"Logical expression comparision","topics":[],"score":213131,"global_rank":252737,"on_pypi":false,"top_pypi":false,"python_rank":41050}]